d-map select:focus-visible,.ex .control select:focus-visible{outline:2px solid #215f50;outline-offset:2px}#household-map .map-controls{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:14px 16px}@media(max-width:540px){#household-map .map-controls{grid-template-columns:1fr}}@media(forced-colors:active){#household-map select,.ex .control select{appearance:auto;background-image:none}} </style><link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://allthingsgwinnett.com/stories/were-gwinnett-county-public-schools-better-in-the-2000s\"><meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://allthingsgwinnett.com/stories/were-gwinnett-county-public-schools-better-in-the-2000s\"><meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://allthingsgwinnett.com/images/gcps-schools-social.jpg\"><meta property=\"og:image:width\" content=\"1200\"><meta property=\"og:image:height\" content=\"630\"><meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\"><meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"Gwinnett County Public Schools, GCPS, Gwinnett schools, Gwinnett school rankings, Gwinnett schools in the 2000s, school performance, graduation rate, SAT scores, reading scores, math scores, student achievement, Georgia school districts, Gwinnett school enrollment\"></head><body><header><b class=\"brand\"><a href=\"/\">All Things Gwinnett</a> / <a href=\"/local-life\">Local Life</a></b><h1>Were Gwinnett County Public Schools better in the 2000s?</h1><div class=\"meta\">Education · By Michael Yang, All Things Gwinnett · November 21, 2026</div><aside class=\"tldr\" aria-label=\"Article summary\"><strong>TL;DR</strong><p>Gwinnett earned its academic reputation in the 2000s. Today, a larger share of GCPS students graduate on time, but districtwide reading scores have fallen behind similar Georgia districts.</p><p>The graduation gains reached schools that once lagged furthest behind. Gwinnett also changed outside school: the share of children living in poverty rose from 6.3% in Census 2000 to an estimated 14.7% in 2020–24.</p></aside><img class=\"hero\" width=\"1672\" height=\"941\" src=\"/images/gcps-school-bus.jpg\" alt=\"A yellow school bus rounds a curve outside a brick school building.\"></header><main><article><p>When I was in Gwinnett schools in the 2000s, our teachers and administrators used to tell us how well we were doing compared with the rest of Georgia. We got to see how our test scores stacked up, too. I remember feeling like we were doing pretty well. Now that I’m a parent here, I wanted to go back to those numbers and see how Gwinnett compared then and where we stand today.</p>\n<p>The public records gave me two answers I can believe at the same time. Gwinnett earned its reputation for strong academics. Today, more students are making it across the graduation stage on time, even as the district has lost ground in reading compared with similar Georgia systems.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-3\" aria-label=\"Source 3\">[3]</a><a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4\">[4]</a></sup></p>\n<p>I’m reading these reports as a parent, not a professional education researcher. A school can change how it teaches a struggling reader. It has much less say over what happens when that child’s family has to move in October. Before drawing conclusions about any one cluster, it helps to look at what changed across Gwinnett.</p>\n<nav class=\"contents\" aria-label=\"In this article\"><strong>In this article</strong><ul><li><a href=\"#history\">Gwinnett in the 2000s<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↓</span></a></li><li><a href=\"#achievement\">Reading and math<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↓</span></a></li><li><a href=\"#households\">Income and migration<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↓</span></a></li><li><a href=\"#grad\">Graduation and SAT scores<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↓</span></a></li><li><a href=\"#gap\">The high school comparison<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↓</span></a></li><li><a href=\"#verdict\">What I take from this<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↓</span></a></li><li><a href=\"#faq\">Frequently asked questions<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↓</span></a></li><li><a href=\"#methods\">Methods and sources<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↓</span></a></li></ul></nav>\n<h2 id=\"history\">What was special about Gwinnett schools in the 2000s?</h2>\n<p>When Gwinnett won the Broad Prize in 2010, the recognition went beyond high test scores. The Associated Press reported that GCPS had outperformed Georgia districts serving families with similar incomes in reading and math the year before. The gap between Black and white students in middle school math had narrowed, too. Part of what people remember as a great school system was its ability to reach students who had been further behind.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-2\" aria-label=\"Source 2\">[2]</a></sup></p>\n<p>Gwinnett was also growing fast. GCPS added more than 28,000 students between 2004 and 2009. A family could stay in the same house while the school around them changed considerably. You can explore how quickly enrollment grew in the chart below.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-1\" aria-label=\"Source 1\">[1]</a></sup></p>\n<figure class=\"ex\" id=\"data-enrollment\"><figcaption>How fast did GCPS grow?</figcaption><small>District enrollment, fiscal years 2004–2010</small><div class=\"control\"></div><div class=\"plot\"></div><details open=\"\"><summary>Explore the data table</summary><div class=\"scroll\"><table><caption>How fast did GCPS grow? · District enrollment, fiscal years 2004–2010</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Year</th><th scope=\"col\">Students</th><th scope=\"col\" data-table-only>YoY growth</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">2004</th><td data-value=\"129014\">129,014</td><td data-value=\"-Infinity\">Baseline</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2005</th><td data-value=\"135392\">135,392</td><td data-value=\"4.943649526407986\">+4.94%</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2006</th><td data-value=\"144598\">144,598</td><td data-value=\"6.799515480973765\">+6.80%</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2007</th><td data-value=\"152043\">152,043</td><td data-value=\"5.148757244221912\">+5.15%</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2008</th><td data-value=\"155618\">155,618</td><td data-value=\"2.351308511407957\">+2.35%</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2009</th><td data-value=\"157219\">157,219</td><td data-value=\"1.0288012954799575\">+1.03%</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2010</th><td data-value=\"159296\">159,296</td><td data-value=\"1.3210871459556415\">+1.32%</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details><div class=\"credit\">Source: <a href=\"#source-1\" aria-label=\"Source 1: Gwinnett County Board of Education, FY2013 financial report, operating statistics, p. 125\">Gwinnett County Board of Education</a></div></figure>\n\n<h2 id=\"achievement\">How are Gwinnett students doing in reading and math?</h2>\n<p>The Education Recovery Scorecard compares Gwinnett students in grades 3–8 with students in five similar Georgia districts. By 2024, Gwinnett still scored above Georgia overall in both reading and math. Its position among those five peers had slipped.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-2\" aria-label=\"Source 2\">[2]</a><a href=\"#source-3\" aria-label=\"Source 3\">[3]</a></sup></p>\n<p>Gwinnett entered 2019 with a math lead over the comparison group. Most of its decline had already happened by 2022, and by 2024 the peers had edged ahead.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-3\" aria-label=\"Source 3\">[3]</a></sup></p>\n<figure class=\"ex\" id=\"data-math\"><figcaption>Math achievement in grades 3–8</figcaption><small>Grade-level equivalents relative to the 2019 national average</small><div class=\"control\"></div><div class=\"plot\"></div><details open=\"\"><summary>Explore the data table</summary><div class=\"scroll\"><table><caption>Math achievement in grades 3–8 · Grade-level equivalents relative to the 2019 national average</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Year</th><th scope=\"col\">GCPS</th><th scope=\"col\">Georgia</th><th scope=\"col\">Peer average</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">2019</th><td>0.69</td><td>−0.18</td><td>0.38</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2022</th><td>0.12</td><td>−0.62</td><td>0.05</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2024</th><td>0.09</td><td>−0.67</td><td>0.14</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details><div class=\"credit\">Source: <a href=\"#source-3\" aria-label=\"Source 3: Education Recovery Scorecard, Gwinnett district report, pp. 1–3\">Education Recovery Scorecard</a></div></figure>\n<p>Reading is the trend I’d want to understand first. Gwinnett and its peers were still fairly close in 2022. Over the next two years, the peers held steady while Gwinnett fell. I’d want to hear from the schools where reading continued to improve as well as those where students struggled.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-3\" aria-label=\"Source 3\">[3]</a></sup></p>\n<figure class=\"ex\" id=\"data-reading\"><figcaption>Reading achievement in grades 3–8</figcaption><small>Grade-level equivalents relative to the 2019 national average</small><div class=\"control\"></div><div class=\"plot\"></div><details open=\"\"><summary>Explore the data table</summary><div class=\"scroll\"><table><caption>Reading achievement in grades 3–8 · Grade-level equivalents relative to the 2019 national average</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Year</th><th scope=\"col\">GCPS</th><th scope=\"col\">Georgia</th><th scope=\"col\">Peer average</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">2019</th><td>0.73</td><td>−0.06</td><td>0.70</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2022</th><td>0.61</td><td>−0.25</td><td>0.68</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2024</th><td>0.20</td><td>−0.36</td><td>0.69</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details><div class=\"credit\">Source: <a href=\"#source-3\" aria-label=\"Source 3: Education Recovery Scorecard, Gwinnett district report, pp. 1–3\">Education Recovery Scorecard</a></div></figure>\n<p>Those are district averages. They can’t tell a parent how well their child reads or identify which feeder schools account for the decline. That takes comparable grade-level results and the number tested at each school.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-3\" aria-label=\"Source 3\">[3]</a><a href=\"#source-8\" aria-label=\"Source 8\">[8]</a><a href=\"#source-11\" aria-label=\"Source 11\">[11]</a></sup></p>\n<h2 id=\"households\">What changed for families outside the classroom?</h2><p>In the Census 2000 figures, roughly one in sixteen Gwinnett children lived below the federal poverty threshold. In the 2020–24 American Community Survey, it was closer to one in seven. Countywide, median household income also lost buying power: about $114,000 in the earlier count versus $87,890 in the later survey, both expressed in 2024 dollars. These measures cover county residents, including families outside GCPS. They give me a different starting point for talking about schools than test scores alone.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-10\" aria-label=\"Source 10\">[10]</a><a href=\"#source-13\" aria-label=\"Source 13\">[13]</a><a href=\"#source-15\" aria-label=\"Source 15\">[15]</a></sup></p><p>Did wealth move north from the Perimeter? The starting map surprised me. In 2000, the county’s two highest-income tracts were in Peachtree Corners near the Chattahoochee. Some of its highest poverty rates were also in southwest Gwinnett, while Buford and Lawrenceville had pockets of poverty of their own. Try comparing areas on either side of Norcross before looking farther north. What looks like one community from the highway can look very different a few streets over.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-10\" aria-label=\"Source 10\">[10]</a></sup></p><figure id=\"household-map\"><figcaption>How have Gwinnett neighborhoods changed?</figcaption><p>Compare two periods. Choose an income or child-poverty measure, then hover, tap or select a tract for details.</p><div class=\"map-controls\"><label>Measure<select><option value=\"0\">Median household income</option><option value=\"1\" selected>Children living in poverty</option></select></label><label>Earlier period<select></select></label><label>Later period<select></select></label><label>2022–23 school zone<select><option value=\"\">No outline</option></select></label></div><div class=\"map-key\"></div><div class=\"map-panels\"></div><label class=\"tract-pick\">Explore a census tract <select></select></label><output aria-live=\"polite\"></output><small>Income uses 2024 dollars. Child poverty is the share of children under 18 for whom poverty status was determined. School outlines are for orientation. Changes are shown only for sufficiently comparable tracts.</small><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to explore the tract maps. Countywide child poverty was 6.3% in Census 2000 and an estimated 14.7% in 2020–24.</p></noscript><div class=\"credit\">Sources: Gwinnett County, U.S. Census Bureau, BLS and school-zone GIS <sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-10\" aria-label=\"Source 10\">[10]</a><a href=\"#source-12\" aria-label=\"Source 12\">[12]</a><a href=\"#source-13\" aria-label=\"Source 13\">[13]</a><a href=\"#source-14\" aria-label=\"Source 14\">[14]</a><a href=\"#source-15\" aria-label=\"Source 15\">[15]</a><a href=\"#source-16\" aria-label=\"Source 16\">[16]</a></sup></div></figure><p>One thing a map can’t tell me is where the same families went. A neighborhood can gain higher-income households because residents earned more, new housing attracted buyers from another state, or families moved in from elsewhere in Gwinnett. I checked the 2020–24 estimates for people earning at least $75,000 who had moved within the county. In just 25 of 220 tracts was the estimate larger than its published margin of error. Even those records don’t identify a previous neighborhood. That’s too little evidence for drawing arrows from Norcross to Suwanee.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-13\" aria-label=\"Source 13\">[13]</a></sup></p><p>For a student, a household move might mean switching schools in October. A change of shift might cost a parent the hour they used to spend reading with a child. Neither experience shows up in a census income bracket. I’d want to hear from families before trying to connect a change on this map to a change in a classroom.</p><p>School meal records offer another perspective. Federal school files historically counted students eligible for free and reduced-price lunch separately. Georgia’s direct-certification figures measure something different, and the state expanded the criteria in 2023–24. At a school offering free meals to everyone, universal access tells us who can eat there; it doesn’t give us every family’s income. That’s why the map uses Census child poverty throughout.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-17\" aria-label=\"Source 17\">[17]</a><a href=\"#source-18\" aria-label=\"Source 18\">[18]</a><a href=\"#source-19\" aria-label=\"Source 19\">[19]</a></sup></p><h2 id=\"grad\">Why graduation and SAT scores need different starting lines</h2>\n<p>If you look up graduation in the old reports, you’ll find what looks like a steep drop in 2011. A footnote explains why: Georgia changed how it counted graduates. That’s why the early and modern figures appear in separate charts.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-1\" aria-label=\"Source 1\">[1]</a></sup></p>\n<figure class=\"ex\" id=\"data-historical-graduation\"><figcaption>Graduation under Georgia’s earlier calculation</figcaption><small>District graduation rate (%)</small><div class=\"control\"></div><div class=\"plot\"></div><details open=\"\"><summary>Explore the data table</summary><div class=\"scroll\"><table><caption>Graduation under Georgia’s earlier calculation · District graduation rate (%)</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Year</th><th scope=\"col\">Rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">2004</th><td>71.9</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2005</th><td>74.8</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2006</th><td>73.8</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2007</th><td>77.3</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2008</th><td>79.1</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2009</th><td>82.0</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2010</th><td>84.7</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details><div class=\"credit\">Source: <a href=\"#source-1\" aria-label=\"Source 1: Gwinnett County Board of Education, FY2013 financial report, operating statistics, p. 125\">Gwinnett County Board of Education</a></div></figure>\n<p>Under the modern four-year measure, GCPS rose from 80.9% in 2019 to 88.3% in 2026. That’s about seven more students finishing on time for every hundred in the cohort. It’s a meaningful gain even though it answers a different question from how well younger students are reading.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4\">[4]</a><a href=\"#source-5\" aria-label=\"Source 5\">[5]</a></sup></p>\n<figure class=\"ex\" id=\"data-four-year-graduation\"><figcaption>Four-year graduation under the cohort calculation</figcaption><small>District graduation rate (%)</small><div class=\"control\"></div><div class=\"plot\"></div><details open=\"\"><summary>Explore the data table</summary><div class=\"scroll\"><table><caption>Four-year graduation under the cohort calculation · District graduation rate (%)</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Class</th><th scope=\"col\">Rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">2016</th><td>79.6</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2017</th><td>80.9</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2018</th><td>81.7</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2019</th><td>80.9</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2020</th><td>83.2</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2021</th><td>82.5</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2022</th><td>83.2</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2023</th><td>81.9</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2024</th><td>84.1</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2025</th><td>85.4</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2026</th><td>88.3</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details><div class=\"credit\">Source: <a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4: GCPS, graduation rates by school and district, classes of 2016–2025\">Gwinnett County Public Schools</a> · <a href=\"#source-5\" aria-label=\"Source 5: GCPS, Class of 2026 graduation release, October 5, 2026\">Gwinnett County Public Schools</a></div></figure>\n<p>The SAT has its own turning point. Starting with the Class of 2024, GCPS offered the test free during the school day to all enrolled students. A student who would have missed a Saturday exam could now take it at school. That makes access broader and comparisons with earlier classes harder. For families asking what a diploma leads to, Georgia also tracks whether graduates enroll in college or enter the workforce.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-6\" aria-label=\"Source 6\">[6]</a><a href=\"#source-7\" aria-label=\"Source 7\">[7]</a><a href=\"#source-9\" aria-label=\"Source 9\">[9]</a></sup></p>\n<h2 id=\"gap\">Which Gwinnett high schools closed the graduation gap?</h2>\n<p>To see what the district average leaves out, I followed the same ten neighborhood high schools for a decade. I grouped them by where their graduation rates stood in 2016 and kept those groups together through 2025. That way, a school that made gains got to keep them in the comparison.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4\">[4]</a></sup></p>\n<figure class=\"ex\" id=\"data-graduation-gap\"><figcaption>Did the same Gwinnett high schools grow closer?</figcaption><small>Four-year graduation rates among neighborhood high schools with results in both 2016 and 2025. Each group keeps the same five schools throughout, weighted equally.</small><dl style=\"font:15px/1.5 Arial,sans-serif\"><dt><strong>Highest five in 2016</strong></dt><dd style=\"margin:0 0 .6em\">North Gwinnett, Brookwood, Grayson, Parkview and Mill Creek.</dd><dt><strong>Lowest five in 2016</strong></dt><dd style=\"margin:0\">Berkmar, Shiloh, Discovery, Norcross and Meadowcreek.</dd></dl><div class=\"control\"></div><div class=\"plot\"></div><details open><summary>Explore the data table</summary><div class=\"scroll\"><table><caption>Graduation rates by starting group and class year (%)</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Year</th><th scope=\"col\">Highest five in 2016</th><th scope=\"col\">Lowest five in 2016</th><th scope=\"col\">GCPS</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">2016</th><td>93.16</td><td>70.96</td><td>79.6</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2017</th><td>93.80</td><td>74.18</td><td>80.9</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2018</th><td>93.48</td><td>76.68</td><td>81.7</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2019</th><td>92.48</td><td>75.96</td><td>80.9</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2020</th><td>93.48</td><td>78.62</td><td>83.2</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2021</th><td>92.20</td><td>77.64</td><td>82.5</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2022</th><td>92.14</td><td>80.68</td><td>83.2</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2023</th><td>93.44</td><td>78.98</td><td>81.9</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2024</th><td>93.68</td><td>80.86</td><td>84.1</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">2025</th><td>93.74</td><td>84.50</td><td>85.4</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details><div class=\"credit\">Source: <a href=\"#source-4\">GCPS school graduation rates</a>. Group averages calculated by All Things Gwinnett.</div></figure>\n<p>The clearest example is Shiloh and Parkview. They started more than 20 percentage points apart. By 2025, both graduated 88.7% of their cohort on time. Across the two five-school groups, the gap narrowed from roughly 22 points to nine.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4\">[4]</a></sup></p>\n<p>That changes how I hear the question “Which schools are carrying Gwinnett?” Berkmar could finish below the district rate and still help move it upward because so many more of its own students were graduating. North Gwinnett could sustain a high rate with little room to climb. Both accomplishments count.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4\">[4]</a></sup></p>\n<p>These are high school graduation results. They don’t rate every elementary and middle school in a feeder cluster. And the chart gives each school equal weight within its group, while the official district rate counts each student. To calculate exactly how much each school contributed, we’d also need its cohort size.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4\">[4]</a><a href=\"#source-11\" aria-label=\"Source 11\">[11]</a></sup></p>\n<h2 id=\"verdict\">So, were Gwinnett schools better when we were kids?</h2>\n<p>I see a real basis for the old reputation. I also see real accomplishments today that nostalgia can obscure. If your family knew Shiloh or Berkmar a decade ago, it matters that substantially more students in those cohorts now finish high school on time. I want my kids to read well and to enjoy reading enough to pick up a book on their own. That’s why Gwinnett losing ground in reading compared with similar districts gets my attention, even though a test score can only tell me about part of that. You shouldn’t have to dismiss one finding to take the other seriously.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-2\" aria-label=\"Source 2\">[2]</a><a href=\"#source-3\" aria-label=\"Source 3\">[3]</a><a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4\">[4]</a></sup></p>\n<p>Our teachers and administrators are working in a different Gwinnett than the one I grew up in. A larger share of children across the county are living in poverty today. At the same time, more Gwinnett students are graduating on time, and some of the biggest gains came at schools that once had the lowest graduation rates. I think our educators are doing a great job with what they have, and that deserves celebrating. I’m still proud of how we did in the 2000s. I’m proud of what our schools are doing for kids today, too.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-10\" aria-label=\"Source 10\">[10]</a><a href=\"#source-13\" aria-label=\"Source 13\">[13]</a><a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4\">[4]</a><a href=\"#source-5\" aria-label=\"Source 5\">[5]</a></sup></p>\n<p>For the parent asking about their own cluster, I’d bring a narrower question to the next school meeting: “What’s happened to reading in my child’s grade over the past few years, and what are teachers seeing that the scores miss?” That conversation would tell me more about my kid’s school than any countywide verdict I could give you here.</p>\n<aside aria-label=\"Related school calendar\"><p>Planning around school breaks, digital learning days or early release? Check the <a href=\"/events/gcps\">Gwinnett County Public Schools calendar</a> for upcoming dates.</p></aside>\n<section id=\"faq\"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><details><summary>Which Gwinnett high schools made the biggest gains as a group?</summary><p>Among neighborhood high schools with graduation results in both 2016 and 2025, the five with the lowest 2016 rates rose from an average of 71.0% to 84.5%. The five highest starters rose from 93.2% to 93.7%.</p><sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4\">[4]</a></sup></details><details><summary>Did the highest-performing schools carry Gwinnett’s graduation rate?</summary><p>The higher-starting schools sustained high graduation rates. The lower-starting group improved faster. Calculating each group’s contribution to the districtwide gain requires its adjusted cohort sizes as well as its graduation rates.</p><sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4\">[4]</a></sup></details><details><summary>Did reading improve in the same Gwinnett clusters?</summary><p>The district-level reading estimates used here cover grades 3–8. They cannot establish which feeder schools drove the decline or whether those schools belong to the same clusters that gained in graduation.</p><sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-3\" aria-label=\"Source 3\">[3]</a></sup></details><details><summary>Does the data show higher-income families moving north within Gwinnett?</summary><p>The four maps show where income and child poverty changed. Establishing that families moved from southwest Gwinnett to northern Gwinnett also requires evidence of their origins and destinations.</p><sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-10\" aria-label=\"Source 10\">[10]</a></sup></details><details><summary>Does this include Buford City Schools?</summary><p>The school results cover Gwinnett County Public Schools. Buford City Schools is a separate district. The county census maps cover a broader population than the GCPS student body.</p><sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-11\" aria-label=\"Source 11\">[11]</a></sup></details></section>\n<h2 id=\"methods\">How I checked the numbers</h2><p>I ranked neighborhood high schools with published four-year graduation rates in both 2016 and 2025 by their 2016 rate. I excluded specialty, alternative and online campuses and schools without a 2016 baseline. Each year’s group figure is the arithmetic mean of the same five published school rates. I calculated changes before rounding. Selecting groups at the extremes of one year can magnify apparent convergence, and high starting rates leave less room for improvement. This is a descriptive comparison rather than an estimate of any school’s effect.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4\">[4]</a></sup></p>\n<p>The district series includes the full adjusted cohort. The reading and math figures are the Scorecard’s rounded estimates on a common scale; zero is the 2019 national average. The historical poverty figures describe county residents, a broader group than GCPS students. I did not link individual families, addresses, incomes or test records. You can find the records behind each comparison below.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-3\" aria-label=\"Source 3\">[3]</a><a href=\"#source-4\" aria-label=\"Source 4\">[4]</a><a href=\"#source-10\" aria-label=\"Source 10\">[10]</a></sup></p><p>The map uses Census 2000 (income in 1999) and nonoverlapping ACS five-year estimates. Income is table B19013; for child poverty I divided the under-18 counts below poverty by the under-18 population with a determined status in B17001. Income figures use annual U.S. CPI-U to convert to 2024 dollars. The two middle periods use 2010 tract geography. For comparisons across vintages, I require the same tract code and overlap covering at least 99% of each polygon, calculated before simplifying the outlines for display. Otherwise the map shows each period separately. ACS figures are estimates; income hover values include the published 90% margin where available. Child-poverty changes are descriptive, not significance tests. The 2022–23 school overlay is a geographic reference for all periods and does not assign Census households to a school.<sup class=\"refs\"><a href=\"#source-10\" aria-label=\"Source 10\">[10]</a><a href=\"#source-12\" aria-label=\"Source 12\">[12]</a><a href=\"#source-13\" aria-label=\"Source 13\">[13]</a><a href=\"#source-14\" aria-label=\"Source 14\">[14]</a><a href=\"#source-15\" aria-label=\"Source 15\">[15]</a><a href=\"#source-16\" aria-label=\"Source 16\">[16]</a></sup></p><section id=\"sources\"><h2>Sources</h2><ol><li id=\"source-1\"><a href=\"https://ted.cviog.uga.edu/financial-documents/sites/default/files/budgetdoc/financial-report/school-district-gwinnett-county-school-district-gwinnett-county-fy2013-financial-report.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gwinnett County Board of Education, FY2013 financial report, operating statistics, p. 125</a></li><li id=\"source-2\"><a href=\"https://www.gpb.org/news/2010/10/19/gwinnett-schools-win-prestigious-prize\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Associated Press, October 2010 Broad Prize coverage, via Georgia Public Broadcasting</a></li><li id=\"source-3\"><a href=\"https://educationrecoveryscorecard.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/report_GA_1302550_gwinnett-county.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Education Recovery Scorecard, Gwinnett district report, pp. 1–3</a></li><li id=\"source-4\"><a href=\"https://www.gcpsk12.org/news-details/~board/news-releases/post/gcps-graduation-rate-reaches-highest-point-in-a-decade-for-second-year-in-a-row\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GCPS, graduation rates by school and district, classes of 2016–2025</a></li><li id=\"source-5\"><a href=\"https://www.gcpsk12.org/get-connected/district-news-and-communication/all-news/gcps-full-news-article/~board/news-releases/post/graduation-rates-are-on-the-rise-across-gcps\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GCPS, Class of 2026 graduation release, October 5, 2026</a></li><li id=\"source-6\"><a href=\"https://www.gcpsk12.org/news-details/~board/news-releases/post/gwinnett-sat-results-top-national-average\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GCPS, Class of 2024 SAT results and school-day testing policy</a></li><li id=\"source-7\"><a href=\"https://resources.finalsite.net/images/v1759332983/fultonschoolsorg/oswe4noo5uojmnyqlssb/SATExecutiveSummary2025.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fulton County Schools, 2025 SAT executive summary, Exhibit 4, metro Atlanta comparison</a></li><li id=\"source-8\"><a href=\"https://goews.georgia.gov/dashboards-data-report-card/downloadable-data\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Georgia education data office, test archives and reporting definitions</a></li><li id=\"source-9\"><a href=\"https://goews.georgia.gov/dashboards-data-report-card/data-dashboards/hs-grad-outcomes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Georgia, High School Graduate Outcomes Dashboard</a></li><li id=\"source-10\"><a href=\"https://www.gwinnettcounty.com/static/departments/planning/Census/census_2000_selected_income_and_poverty_status_characteristics.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gwinnett County, Census 2000 income and poverty maps, especially maps 8–9</a></li><li id=\"source-11\"><a href=\"https://www.gcpsk12.org/schools/clusters/list-of-schools-by-cluster\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GCPS, schools and feeder patterns by cluster</a></li><li id=\"source-12\"><a href=\"https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs/summary_file/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">U.S. Census Bureau, ACS sequence-based summary files (2006–10 and 2011–15)</a></li><li id=\"source-13\"><a href=\"https://api.censusreporter.org/1.0/data/show/acs2024_5yr?table_ids=B19013,B17001,B07010&amp;geo_ids=05000US13135\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">U.S. Census Bureau, 2020–24 ACS tables B19013, B17001 and B07010, accessed through Census Reporter</a></li><li id=\"source-14\"><a href=\"https://www2.census.gov/geo/tiger/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">U.S. Census Bureau, TIGER/Line and cartographic boundaries</a></li><li id=\"source-15\"><a href=\"https://data.bls.gov/timeseries/CUUR0000SA0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers</a></li><li id=\"source-16\"><a href=\"https://services3.arcgis.com/jEYjQXUX2JTVoctH/ArcGIS/rest/services/High_School_Zone_22_23/FeatureServer/21\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gwinnett high school attendance zones, 2022–23 GIS layer</a></li><li id=\"source-17\"><a href=\"https://nces.ed.gov/pubs2003/2003604.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">National Center for Education Statistics, 2000–01 school file documentation</a></li><li id=\"source-18\"><a href=\"https://goews.georgia.gov/directcert\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Georgia education data office, direct certification and the Medicaid definition change</a></li><li id=\"source-19\"><a href=\"https://www.gcpsk12.org/about-us/divisions-and-teams/facilities-and-operations/school-nutrition-program/provision-2-breakfast\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GCPS, community eligibility and school meal provisions</a></li></ol></section></article></main><script>function enableTooltips(f,data,idx,names){\n const plot=f.querySelector('.plot'),svg=plot.querySelector('svg'),dots=[...svg.querySelectorAll('circle')].slice(0,data.length);\n svg.removeAttribute('aria-hidden');svg.setAttribute('role','group');svg.setAttribute('aria-label',f.querySelector('figcaption').textContent);\n svg.querySelectorAll('circle title').forEach(t=>t.remove());\n const tip=document.createElement('div');tip.className='point-tip';tip.hidden=true;plot.append(tip);\n const xs=dots.map(dot=>Number(dot.getAttribute('cx')));\n data.forEach((row,i)=>{\n const left=i?(xs[i-1]+xs[i])/2:48,right=i+1<xs.length?(xs[i]+xs[i+1])/2:600;\n const hit=document.createElementNS(svg.namespaceURI,'rect');\n for(const [k,v] of Object.entries({x:left,y:10,width:right-left,height:177,fill:'transparent',tabindex:0,role:'group'}))hit.setAttribute(k,v);\n const text=idx.map(j=>{const n=row[j];if(f.id==='data-graduation-gap')return names[j]+': '+n.toFixed(2)+'%';if(names[j]==='Rate')return 'Graduation rate: '+n.toFixed(1)+'%';if(names[j]==='Students')return 'Students: '+n.toLocaleString('en-US');return names[j]+': '+n.toFixed(2)+' grade equivalents';});\n const label=[row[0],...text].join('\\n');hit.setAttribute('aria-label',label.replaceAll('\\n',', '));\n function show(){tip.textContent=label;tip.hidden=false;tip.style.left=Math.max(0,Math.min(plot.clientWidth-tip.offsetWidth,(xs[i]/620)*plot.clientWidth-tip.offsetWidth/2))+'px';}\n function hide(){tip.hidden=true}\n hit.addEventListener('pointerenter',show);hit.addEventListener('pointerdown',show);hit.addEventListener('focus',show);hit.addEventListener('blur',hide);hit.addEventListener('keydown',e=>{if(e.key==='Escape')hide()});svg.append(hit);\n });plot.onpointerleave=()=>{tip.hidden=true};\n}\nfor(const f of document.querySelectorAll('.ex')){\n const table=f.querySelector('table'), head=[...table.tHead.rows[0].cells], rows=[...table.tBodies[0].rows], data=rows.map(r=>[...r.cells].map(c=>Number(c.dataset.value ?? c.textContent.replace('−','-')))),names=head.filter(c=>!c.hasAttribute('data-table-only')).map(c=>c.textContent);\n const ctl=f.querySelector('.control'), multi=names.length>2;\n ctl.innerHTML='<label>View <select></select></label>'; const sel=ctl.querySelector('select');\n for(const [i,n] of (multi?['All comparisons',...names.slice(1)]:['All years',...data.map(r=>r[0])]).entries())sel.add(new Option(n,i));\n function draw(){const pick=+sel.value,idx=names.slice(1).map((_,i)=>i+1).filter(i=>!multi||!pick||i===pick), colors=['#17634f','#ad4c26','#325c96'];const groupRates=f.id==='data-graduation-gap',lo=groupRates?0:multi?-1:0,hi=groupRates?100:multi?1:names[1]==='Students'?180000:100;const X=x=>65+(x-data[0][0])/(data.at(-1)[0]-data[0][0])*515,Y=y=>170-(y-lo)/(hi-lo)*140;\n let s='';for(let i=0;i<=4;i++){const v=lo+(hi-lo)*i/4;s+='<path stroke=\"#d9dfda\" d=\"M65 '+Y(v)+'H580\"/><text x=\"55\" y=\"'+(Y(v)+4)+'\" text-anchor=\"end\">'+v.toLocaleString()+'</text>'}\n for(const j of idx){const col=colors[j-1];s+='<polyline fill=\"none\" stroke=\"'+col+'\" stroke-width=\"3\" points=\"'+data.map(r=>X(r[0])+','+Y(r[j])).join(' ')+'\"/>';data.forEach((r,i)=>s+='<circle cx=\"'+X(r[0])+'\" cy=\"'+Y(r[j])+'\" r=\"'+(!multi&&pick===i+1?7:4)+'\" fill=\"'+col+'\"><title>'+r[0]+' · '+names[j]+': '+r[j]+'</title></circle>');}\n data.forEach((r,i)=>{if(data.length<9||i%2===0)s+='<text x=\"'+X(r[0])+'\" y=\"192\" text-anchor=\"middle\">'+r[0]+'</text>'});\n f.querySelector('.plot').innerHTML='<svg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 205\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"14\">'+s+'</svg>'+(multi?idx.map(j=>'<span style=\"font:14px Arial;margin-right:12px;color:'+colors[j-1]+'\">● '+names[j]+'</span>').join(''):'');rows.forEach((r,i)=>r.hidden=!multi&&pick!==0&&pick!==i+1);head.forEach((_,j)=>{const hide=multi&&pick&&j!==0&&j!==pick;head[j].hidden=!!hide;rows.forEach(r=>r.cells[j].hidden=!!hide)});enableTooltips(f,data,idx,names);}\n head.forEach((h,j)=>{const b=document.createElement('button');b.textContent=h.textContent;b.title='Sort by '+b.textContent;h.replaceChildren(b);b.onclick=()=>{const asc=h.getAttribute('aria-sort')!=='ascending';head.forEach(c=>c.removeAttribute('aria-sort'));h.setAttribute('aria-sort',asc?'ascending':'descending');[...rows].sort((x,y)=>(data[rows.indexOf(x)][j]-data[rows.indexOf(y)][j])*(asc?1:-1)).forEach(r=>table.tBodies[0].append(r))}});sel.onchange=draw;draw();}\n</script><script src=\"/features/gcps-household-map.js\"></script></body></html>"